TI Damage mechanism identification in additive manufactured extruded polymer via acoustic emission technique, experimental testing and pattern recognition models
SO ENGINEERING FAILURE ANALYSIS
LA English
DT Article
DE Artificial intelligence; Acoustic emission; Material extrusion; Failure characterization; Structural integrity
ID SIGNALS
AB This work presents a novel framework for identifying damage mechanisms in material extrusion-based additive manufacturing parts, combining acoustic emission (AE) analysis with unsupervised and hybrid supervised learning techniques. The main objective is to characterize failure modes in polylactic acid specimens and demonstrate the practical potential of data-driven AE analysis for early damage detection and quantification in polymeric additive manufacturing. Extending the current state of the art, the novelty of this study lies in the exploration of multiple clustering algorithms through two approaches: (i) unsupervised learning comparing k-means and density-based spatial clustering of noise-based applications (DBSCAN) combined with fast Fourier transform (FFT) for feature extraction and (ii) a hybrid supervised deep learning approach using a convolutional neural network (CNN) trained on spectrograms from the continuous wavelet transform (CWT). While these approaches have been applied in fields such as composite materials, this is the first work to transfer and validate their use in extrusion-based polymer specimens. The experimental campaign based on different stack configurations of additive extruded samples provides ground-truth fracture observations for model assessment. As a result, DBSCAN proved to be effective in the classification process, identifying two distinct clusters: one with relatively low frequencies up to 260 kHz and another with high frequencies between 270 and 390 kHz. The correlations between these groups and the failure mechanisms were established by visually inspecting the fractured specimens. On the other hand, the hybrid approach combined the labels obtained from the DBSCAN model and the time-frequency features extracted from the signal spectrograms, achieving results consistent with the unsupervised approach. Finally, after enhancing the model performance through successive revisions via experimental correlation of different data-driven analysis pipelines, the present research offers a practical, rapid, and non-destructive tool for in-situ damage detection in polymeric additive manufacturing, contributing to structural health monitoring and process optimization.
