TI An interpretable machine learning approach for quantitative structure-permeation prediction of polymeric microneedles
SO CHEMICAL ENGINEERING SCIENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE Microneedles; Polymer informatics; Transdermal delivery; Machine learning; Permeation performance
ID TRANSDERMAL DRUG-DELIVERY; DISSOLVING MICRONEEDLES
AB Microneedle (MN) technology represents a promising strategy for minimally invasive transdermal drug delivery. However, rational design of polymeric MNs remains challenging due to the absence of quantitative models linking polymer structures to permeation performance. This study proposes an integrated framework combining structural information of drugs and polymers to predict the in vitro permeation behavior of dissolving MNs (DMNs). A novel polymer representation, termed the Polymer Image Structural Descriptor (PISD), was developed to encode polymer architecture at the substructural level. By converting polymer SMILES (PSMILES) into twodimensional Boolean matrices and employing a convolutional neural network (CNN), PISD enables automatic extraction of local structural patterns and feature interactions. The PISD, together with drug molecular descriptors generated by RDKit, DMN properties, and skin type, was used to train multiple machine learning models. Among them, the Categorical Boosting (CatBoost) model incorporating PISD achieved the highest predictive accuracy (R2 = 0.869) on the independent test set. Interpretability analysis using SHapley Additive ex-Planations (SHAP) and SHAP-weighted activation maps (SHAP-CAM) indicated that both drug and polymer features jointly influence permeation behavior. This framework provides a quantitative and interpretable structure-performance modeling platform for the rational design of polymer-based microneedle systems, highlighting the potential of machine learning for data-driven materials and process design in transdermal delivery applications.
