TI Boosting polymeric hollow-fiber membrane gas separation process by evaluating its performance utilizing AI algorithms
SO SEPARATION SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY
LA English
DT Article
DE Artificial intelligence; machine learning; artificial neural networks; membrane separation; hollow-fiber membrane module; modeling and simulation
ID SENSITIVITY-ANALYSIS; PERMEATORS
AB Membrane gas separation is an essential process in various industrial fields due to the several advantages over other gas separation processes, including simplicity, small footprint, and reduced energy consumption. Evaluating the performance of membrane gas separation module is crucial for boosting the process efficiency. In particular, hollow fiber membrane modules are favored for their high area to volume ratio, and high-pressure resilience. Hence, it essential to adequately assess hollow fiber membrane performance to optimize process and improve cost efficiency. However, experimental trials are costly, and the existing theoretical approaches are often complex, and require extensive processing and a solid mathematical background. Hence, there is a pressing need for innovative solutions, such as the application of artificial intelligence (AI) in evaluating membrane gas separation performance. In particular, the implementation of machine learning (ML), including Artificial Neural Networks (ANN), has the potential to be used in the evaluation of polymeric hollow-fiber membrane gas separation performance. In this work, various ANN configurations are studied and their accuracy in modeling membrane gas separation process performance is evaluated. Findings show that an ANN with five hidden layers of four nodes achieves optimal results for both permeate purity and stagecut, with root mean squared errors of 0.0230 and 0.0301 respectively, and R2 values of 0.9824 and 0.9781, respectively. This model facilitates simulations of the process in a wide range of operating conditions and helps identifying the optimal parameters. Particularly, permeate to feed pressure ratio, membrane selectivity, and feed flow were found to be the most influential parameters affecting the process performance, by the mean of permeate purity, and stagecut. Moreover, using AI reduces the complexities associated with traditional theoretical methods, however, with valid results from physical perspective. This work represents a pioneering effort to employ AI for evaluating membrane gas separation performance and presents a solid startup for a deeper understanding of the influencing parameters in this potential process.
