TI Thermally Stable Organic Synaptic Transistors Using a High-Tg Polymer Electret
SO ADVANCED INTELLIGENT SYSTEMS
LA English
DT Article
DE cyclic olefin copolymer; neuromorphic computing; organic field-effect transistor; polymer electret; synaptic transistor
ID GLASS-TRANSITION TEMPERATURE; SURFACE; ENERGY
AB Polymer electrets are widely used as charge-trapping layers in organic synaptic transistors (OSTs). However, common polymer electrets such as polystyrene, polyvinyl alcohol, and poly (2-vinyl naphthalene) have glass-transition temperatures (T g) below 150 degrees C, limiting their use in high temperature processing or operating conditions. Cyclic olefin copolymer (COC) has attracted interest as a high-T g polymer electret owing to its excellent thermal robustness (T g 185 degrees C), yet its inherently low trap density restricts its applicability in polymer electret-based OSTs. Here, the trap density of COC is effectively enhanced through UV-ozone (UVO) treatment, which introduces polar carbonyl groups and defect sites that act as additional charge-trapping states, enabling thermally stable synaptic transistor operation. The resulting devices exhibit key synaptic behaviors, including excitatory and inhibitory postsynaptic currents, paired-pulse facilitation and depression, short- to long-term memory transition, and long-term potentiation and depression. Optimized devices demonstrate improved linearity in synaptic weight updates with nonlinearity factors of alpha p = 0.59 and alpha d = -0.47. When implemented in a multilayer perceptron-based artificial neural network for handwritten digit recognition, the devices achieve an accuracy of 93.2% after 100 training epochs. These results demonstrate that UVO-treated COC is a promising high-T g polymer electret for thermally robust OSTs and highlight its potential for neuromorphic and artificial intelligence applications in high-temperature environments.
