TI Re-modelling of drug-carrier molecular interaction for polymer- and lipid-based nanocarriers using K-nearest neighbors machine learning
SO RSC PHARMACEUTICS
LA English
DT Article; Early Access
ID PREDICTION
AB Machine learning (ML) applications are revolutionizing the pharmaceutical industry. This study evaluates the application of K-nearest neighbors (K-NN) as an efficient ML method for optimizing molecular docking binding energies of drugs on their carriers. By optimizing these energy values, which reflect on the loading efficiencies, the delivery performance of drug delivery carriers can be maximized. Datasets of drugs that were loaded on tripalmitin-based solid lipid nanoparticles (SLNs) and polymeric nanoparticles (NPs) and modeled using artificial neural networks (ANN), another ML method, were gathered from the literature and re-modeled using K-NN. The main drug descriptors (x-variables) used for modeling were molecular weight (MWT), partition coefficient (X log P), fragment complexity (FC), and topological polar surface area (TPSA), whilst the response y-variable was the binding energy (Delta G). The JMP software was utilized to create the models and generate the characteristic plots, such as the contour and 3D response surface ones. Accuracy of the K-NN model predictions was recorded according to test points, and a comparison was made to ANN results. For SLNs, the average percentage bias for the ANN model was 5.59%, whereas that for the K-NN model was only 2.66%. Moreover, for the polymeric NPs, the reported average percentage bias for the ANN and K-NN models was 11.83% and 10.11%, respectively. Overall, this study provides strong evidence that K-NN can be used as an efficient ML method for modelling and predicting important critical quality attributes or responses related to advanced drug delivery systems such as tripalmitin-based SLNs and polymeric NPs.
