TI A flexible wearable single-electrode triboelectric sensor based on self-healing ionic elastomer for reliable underwater motion sensing
SO CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Self-healing ionic elastomer; Triboelectric nanogenerator; Wearable sensor; Underwater motion sensing; Deep learning
AB Reliable wearable sensing in underwater environments remains challenging due to severe signal instability, motivating the development of new device design strategies. Here, we report a flexible wearable single-electrode triboelectric sensor based on the self-healing ionic elastomer electrode. The ionic elastomer electrode is constructed from a liquid-free poly (ionic liquid), exhibits high environmental stability, strong underwater adhesion, large stretchability with a maximum strain approaching 1000% and rapid self-healing within 8 h. This wearable triboelectric sensor integrates intrinsically stable electrode materials with a dedicated castable encapsulation, enabling highly stable electrical outputs under prolonged water exposure (up to 7 days) and across varying water depths (0.2-1 m). In addition, the sensor demonstrates a fast response capability at the sub-50-millisecond level, outperforming most reported flexible sensors in real-time sensing. The device enables wearable human motion recognition when combined with a convolutional neural network-bidirectional long short-term memory deep learning model, achieving an overall recognition accuracy of 98.74% for nine distinct human motions. This device is subsequently employed to design a simplified information transmission system as a typical application of underwater motion sensing. The system adopts Morse code encoding and successfully decoded the SOS signals tapped by 10 volunteers. These results further demonstrate the great potential of the designed triboelectric sensor based on self-healing ionic elastomer electrodes in reliable underwater sensing applications.
