TI A new method to find the material constants for Goldberg's strain-rate dependent constitutive model: an application in automotive polymeric instrument panel
SO MATERIALS RESEARCH EXPRESS
LA English
DT Article
DE strain-rate; viscoplasticity; constitutive model; head impact; automotive; polymer
ID BEHAVIOR
AB It has been demonstrated that, considering the strain-rate effect in deformation behavior of the polymers and polymeric-based composites is a 'must' for transient dynamic simulations. Goldberg's constitutive model which was developed on the basis of Bodner-Partom's viscoplastic model for metals, is a conventional method for describing the strain-rate dependent deformation of polymers. The Goldberg's proposed method for identification of the material parameters has a couple of limitations, leading this model to be unusable or costly and time-consuming in many cases, such as unavailability of pure shear test data. In the present work, these drawbacks are identified and eliminated by means of a new algorithm for determination of the material parameters. This evolutionary algorithm is based on minimization of an error function which compares the estimated stress-strain curves to the experimental results. Moreover, a new concept is stated in this paper; training and testing of a constitutive model, analogous to what is well-known in the artificial neural networks. The presented method is implemented on two materials, Epoxy resin PR520 and PVC. Validity of the trained model is investigated using experimental test results for PVC. Subsequently, a VUMAT subroutine is developed for ABAQUS/Explicit finite element product. Finally, the head impact of an occupant to the instrument panel of a hatch-back vehicle is simulated using finite element method and the novel subroutine.
