TI Performance evaluation of high-voltage polymer insulators under artificial cement dust contamination: a real-time prediction model and fangled methodology
SO MEASUREMENT
LA English
DT Article
DE High-voltage polymer insulators; Flashover voltage prediction; Artificial cement dust contamination; MFCNN; Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique; (SMOTE); ITMDBO; Surface conductivity analysis
AB High-voltage polymeric insulators play a very crucial role in power transmission. But the cement dust contamination is a significant reliability problem. The pollutants such as calcium sulphate, sodium chloride, and kaolin may affect the surface conductivity of the insulator increasing the leakage current. In this work, a machine-learning approach is proposed to measure the performance of the insulator affected by the cement dust contamination. The real-time prediction model is accurately created by utilizing the environmental data for one year, consisting of temperature, solar radiation, humidity, elevation, dew point, wind speed, line voltage and service period. Similarly, the approaches also comprise data acquisition and preprocessing data based on critical parameters like voltage levels (33 kV to 400 kV), contamination types and insulator properties like leakage current, flashover voltage, Normalized Salt Deposit Density, and equivalent salt deposit density. Leakage current waveform analysis aids feature extraction, which is followed by processing through a multi-granularity fusion convolutional neural network (MFCNN). The synthetic minority over-sampling method is used for data normalization and class balancing to increase model stability. The Inter-Twinned Mutation-based Dragon Boat Optimization (ITMDBO) is also used to optimize prediction models in a decision support system for maintenance and cost optimization. Surface conductivity and leakage current are strongly correlated, with maximum values of about 2 A, according to the results. The suggested approach improves insulator performance and measures the impact of contamination.
