TI Data-driven multi-objective optimization of 3D-printed biodegradable polymer optical fiber preforms
SO ENGINEERING RESEARCH EXPRESS
LA English
DT Article
DE polymer optical fibers; 3D printing; machine learning; intelligent optimization
ID SURFACE
AB Biodegradable polymer optical fibers (POFs) are attracting increasing interest for flexible, lightweight, and sustainable photonic applications. Three-dimensional (3D) printing provides a promising route for fabricating such fibers and their preforms, but the strong coupling among printing parameters still limits reliable control of optical, surface, and mechanical performance. Here, we develop an interpretable data-driven framework for optimizing the fused deposition modeling fabrication of polylactic acid (PLA)-based POF preforms. Five process parameters-layer height, printing speed, nozzle temperature, platform temperature, and extrusion rate-are used as input variables, while optical transmittance, surface roughness, and tensile strength are selected as target responses. A neural-network (NN) surrogate is benchmarked against response surface methodology, support vector regression, random forest, and Gaussian process regression under the same data split and validation protocol. The NN is retained because it provides the best test-set performance, with R2 values above 0.85 for all three outputs and the lowest overall prediction error. SHapley Additive exPlanations further reveal how parameter interactions govern the optical-mechanical trade-offs. Coupling the NN surrogate with the non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm II and entropy-weighted technique for order preference by similarity to an ideal solution yields a well-distributed Pareto front and identifies a balanced solution (P4). Compared with randomly selected baseline parameter sets, P4 increases transmittance by 6.13%, reduces surface roughness by 65.55%, and improves tensile strength by 59.01%, while achieving the lowest preform transmission loss of 1.85 +/- 0.01 dB cm-1. The proposed framework offers an efficient route for high-performance 3D-printed biodegradable POF preforms and reduces the required experimental trials by approximately 95% relative to a full-factorial campaign.
