TI Corotational dynamic modeling and PSO-DNN framework for hard-magnetic soft elastomers
SO INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF MECHANICAL SCIENCES
LA English
DT Article
DE Hard-magnetic soft beam; Hard-magnetic soft shell; Dynamic modeling; Corotational formulation; PSO-DNN framework; Signed distance field
ID BEAM ELEMENTS; INSTABILITY; FRICTION; CHOICE; SHELLS; ROBOT
AB Hard-magnetic soft (HMS) elastomers have gained widespread application in various fields, particularly in biomedical engineering and robotics, due to their flexibility and remote actuation capability. The development of an accurate dynamic model is crucial for capturing the rapid response and large deformation characteristics. This paper presents three-dimensional dynamic models for hard-magnetic soft beam (HMSB) and shell (HMSS) based on corotational (CR) formulation. The generalized force and tangent stiffness, incorporating magnetic effects, are derived to achieve stable implicit time integration. Additionally, to address the complex working environments, contact detection and contact force are integrated into the modeling process. A discrete signed distance field (SDF) is constructed in advance to improve the efficiency of contact detection. Furthermore, a novel framework combining particle swarm optimization (PSO) and deep neural network (DNN), termed PSO-DNN, is proposed to efficiently solve the inverse problems related to magnetic field calculations. Its performance is compared with a straightforward inverse DNN method where the input and output are interchanged. Finally, the developed dynamic models and PSO-DNN framework are validated through comprehensive benchmark tests, demonstrating excellent agreement with theoretical solutions for HMSB critical magnetic flux density (0.73% error), strong consistency with experimental data for HMSS bending under different constraints (errors below 10% and 5%), and high precision in inverse problem solutions (error below 0.6%) for HMS elastomers workspace. Successful simulations of medical applications, including guidewire navigation, drug delivery, and steering robots, demonstrate the potential applicability of the proposed methods.
