TI Longterm dynamics of plastisphere succession and polymer degradation mechanisms in marine environments: A 12-month in-situ study with machine learning applications
SO JOURNAL OF CLEANER PRODUCTION
LA English
DT Article
DE Marine plastics degradation; Biofilm dynamics; Cyanobacteria colonization; Machine learning prediction
ID PLASTICS; BACTERIAL; BLOOMS
AB The escalating global crisis of marine plastic pollution underscores the urgent need to investigate longterm weathering mechanisms and associated ecological risks under environmental conditions. In this study, a 12month in-situ exposure experiment was conducted in a subtropical marine environment to systematically elucidate the degradation dynamics of polypropylene (PP) and polylactic acid (PLA) and their interactions with microbial communities. Results demonstrate polymer-specific aging patterns and biofilm-enhanced risks, revealing that cyanobacteria (harmful algal bloom initiators) preferentially colonize persistent PP (12-15% abundance) over biodegradable PLA (0.22-0.26%). The persistence of PP in the environment enables stable cyanobacterial biofilms to act as longterm dispersal vectors for algal bloom initiation. In contrast, PLA surfaces were enriched with hydrolytic bacteria (e.g., Firmicutes), and this colonization was associated with enhanced mass loss and secondary microplastic formation. Furthermore, seasonal variations exerted limited control over dominant plastisphere taxa, with microbial communities on homogeneous plastics converging over time. Polymer type dominated plastisphere assembly, followed by exposure duration and physical form. Moreover, a deep convolutional neural network (CNN)-based machine learning (ML) model was developed to predict the weathering degree of plastics, achieving a highly reliable accuracy of 85.3 +/- 0.8% by extracting image features of the weathered plastic surface, offering a novel paradigm for longterm ecological risk assessment. This work indicates that the short-term experiments are insufficient for predicting the fate of plastics and highlights the necessity of integrating multimodal data into extended observational frameworks and direct measures of microbial activity to evaluate ecological risks more accurately.
