TI Performance analysis and strength prediction of polymer-modified manufactured sand concrete using artificial bee colony optimization algorithm
SO MATERIALS TODAY COMMUNICATIONS
LA English
DT Article
DE Manufactured sand concrete; ABC-BPNN; Isothermal calorimetry; Pore structure; Mix ratio optimization; Mechanical properties
ID CHLORIDE PENETRATION; FLY-ASH; DURABILITY; HYDRATION; RESISTANCE; SILANES
AB In harsh environments such as western saline soils and coastal zones of China, traditional concrete deteriorates rapidly under SO42- and Cl- attacks, leading to reduced service life and costly maintenance. To address this, a dual-driven mix design method is proposed, integrating heuristic algorithm-assisted optimization and physical performance regulation to enhance multi-component, low-carbon polymer-modified manufactured sand concrete (MMSC). The applicability of fly ash (FA), slag powder (SP), granite powder (GP), concrete erosion inhibitor (CEI), and polycarboxylate superplasticizer (PBS) in MMSC was systematically evaluated. Mechanical performance was assessed through flexural, compressive, and splitting tensile strengths. Isothermal calorimetry and MIP analysis were conducted to reveal the synergistic mechanism of CEI and supplementary cementitious materials at different ages. To identify influential factors and optimize the input structure of the model, correlation analysis and principal component analysis (PCA) were conducted to investigate the effects of raw material components on mechanical behavior. Subsequently, a backpropagation neural network model optimized by artificial bee colony algorithm (ABC-BPNN) was established. A 10-22-3 multi-input, multi-output neural architecture was adopted, utilizing 333 groups of experimental data, with 60 % used for training and 40 % for validation. The model achieved high-efficiency prediction and inverse mix proportioning. Results showed that incorporating 5 % CEI effectively regulated the hydration process, increased cumulative heat release (169.87 J.g(-1)), and refined the late-age pore structure, significantly improving the 28d strength of MMSC at w/b = 0.38. When combined with 15 % SP, a synergistic effect was observed at 28 d, particularly the total amount of gel pores and transitional pores increased to 81.9 %. Compared with BPNN (R-2 = 0.89), the ABC-BPNN model exhibited superior prediction accuracy and generalizability, providing an effective tool for guiding MMSC optimization. This study proposes a data-driven paradigm for designing low-carbon, high-performance cement-based materials, offering valuable theoretical and engineering implications for waste utilization and sustainable material development.
