TI Data-driven machine learning meta-analysis of process-property relationships in polymer additive manufacturing: A case study on FFF-printed PEEK
SO JOURNAL OF MANUFACTURING PROCESSES
LA English
DT Review
DE Additive manufacturing; Fused filament fabrication; PEEK; Machine learning; Sensitivity analysis
ID MECHANICAL-PROPERTIES; PROCESS PARAMETERS; QUALITY; SURFACE; PARTS
AB Polymers are widely used in additive manufacturing (AM) technologies including fused filament fabrication (FFF), where process parameters significantly influence the structure, properties, and performance of finished parts. Establishing process-property relationships in this context is particularly challenging due to the high dimensionality of the process space, the small size and sparsity of available data, and the high level of noise in published literature. In addition, the literature often reports incomplete and heterogeneous process metadata, which complicates cross-study analysis, generalization and interpretability. This paper presents a data-driven machine learning framework and case-study meta-analysis focused on reported process-property measurements of FFF parts with polyether-ether-ketone (PEEK). A dataset of 205 data points was curated into a machinelearning-ready database from 15 different studies to examine the effects of nine commonly reported printing parameters on the ultimate tensile strength (UTS) of FFF-printed coupons. To uncover process-property correlations, surrogate neural network (NN) modeling and global sensitivity analysis (GSA) are applied. To improve robustness and support learning under data-limited conditions, an ensemble surrogate modeling strategy is adopted, involving the training of over 1200 NNs and selection of the top five models based on test R2 of approximately 83.2%. To support model selection, NN performance was also benchmarked against alternative surrogate models such as Gaussian process regression. The ensemble model is then used for Sobol-based sensitivity analysis. Results show that while UTS is sensitive to printing parameters such as infill percentage and printing speed, total-effect indices indicate that interaction effects contribute to the overall sensitivity for several parameters within the pooled literature-derived dataset, providing an explainable screening of cross-study parameter importance. This study highlights the value of data-driven methods in quantifying uncertainties and discovering correlations from sparse, heterogeneous literature-derived datasets in additive manufacturing.
