PT J
AU Alhaddad, W
   He, MJ
   Halabi, Y
   Almajhali, KYM
AF Alhaddad, Wael
   He, Minjuan
   Halabi, Yahia
   Almajhali, Khalil Yahya Mohammed
TI Optimizing the material and printing parameters of the additively manufactured fiber-reinforced polymer composites using an artificial neural network model and artificial bee colony algorithm
SO STRUCTURES
LA English
DT Article
DE Fiber-reinforced polymer; 3D printing; Artificial neural network; Optimization; Artificial Bee Colony algorithm
ID THERMOPLASTIC COMPOSITES; CONTINUOUS CARBON; WOOD FIBER; PERFORMANCE
AB Fiber-reinforced polymer composite (FRP) has always been attracting the attention of researchers due to its multiple applications as a primary material for manufacturing structural elements and parts or as a reinforcing material for the structural elements. Controlling the quality of the additively manufactured FRP is critical to guarantee an FRP material of high performance for different applications. Therefore, in this research, a machine learning model based on data collected from experimental studies was developed using the artificial neural network (ANN) modeling method to control the quality of FRP manufactured by fused deposition modeling (FDM) printing technology. ANN model predicts the tensile properties, particularly ultimate tensile strength (UTS), of the FRP as a function of 7 material and printing parameters, which are fiber tensile strength, polymer tensile strength, layer thickness, fiber volume fraction, fiber form, infill pattern, and fiber orientation. A parametric study was conducted to confirm the credibility of the developed ANN model in representing the pairwise relationship between each input parameter and the predicted UTS. The UTS of the FRP was maximized by optimizing the printing and material parameters by using the artificial bee colony (ABC) algorithm. Several case studies were conducted to prove the efficiency of the coupled ANN model and optimization algorithm. The ANN modeling method and ABC algorithm were codified and applied using MATLAB software. The results showed that the developed ANN model could accurately predict the UTS of FRP. The parametric study confirmed that the developed ANN model was valid for representing the pairwise relationships between the input parameters and the predicted UTS. Also, it was found that the fiber properties, fiber form, fiber orientation, and fiber volume fraction have the highest influence on the UTS of FRP manufactured by FDM compared with other parameters. Furthermore, the ABC optimization algorithm can design the input parameters (i.e., material and printing parameters) to obtain an FRP with maximum UTS. For future studies, it is recommended to include more input parameters, such as printing speed, nozzle temperature, and infill density, in addition to outputs, such as impact and flexural properties.
