TI Robust flexible sensors, wearable technologies, and artificial intelligence: Elastomeric composite prospects
SO JOURNAL OF ALLOYS AND COMPOUNDS
LA English
DT Review
DE Flexible Electronics; Wearable Technology; Artificial Intelligence; Healthcare; Elastomer Composites
ID HAPTICS
AB The key relationship among sensing, wearables, and their AI prospects has been the focus recently. These technologies have found new-generation material applications that enable the development of smart sensing and robust intelligent systems. The typical gauge factor ranges from 1 to > 10(4), and the stretchability ranges from 50 to > 1000% for elastomer-based sensors. Similarly, the typical AI model accuracy ranges from 70% to 95% for basic regression models, 80-95% for machine learning models, and 90-98% for deep learning models. So, the first part of this review paper provides insights into fabrication tools to obtain these sensors based on elastomer composites. These tools are the molding, layer-by-layer assembly, and roll-to-roll technologies. The section is followed by a second section that details insights on the sensors based on elastomeric composites. This section explores the literature for different types, such as thermal sensors, pressure sensors, and strain sensors. These sensors act as interfaces between real-world applications and electronic systems. These elastomer composite-based sensors are also promising as wearable sensors due to their unique properties. These unique features are their stretchability, wearability, lightweight, cost-effectiveness, and biocompatibility. Finally, the challenges in the scalability of these sensors are reported. These challenges are poor filler dispersion, low interfacial stability, and low durability. The final part of the paper explores the use of edge AI for AI models. These models are Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and neural network prospects. This section also reports the advantages and challenges of these AI tools for a better understanding of these elastomer composite-based sensors. Moreover, these models transform the raw data into robust insights like recognition gestures. Finally, the review paper also provides prospects of emphasis on health monitoring, strain sensing, tactile interaction, and data interpretation.
