TI Pore structure analysis and prediction of characteristics by metaheuristic algorithm of manufactured sand polymer-modified repair mortar
SO CONSTRUCTION AND BUILDING MATERIALS
LA English
DT Article
DE Manufactured sand; Polymer-modified repair mortar; Mechanical properties; Impermeability; Pore structure; GWO-BPNN; Mix ratio optimization
ID FLY-ASH; CONCRETE; LIMESTONE; IMPACT; PERFORMANCE; REPLACEMENT; DESIGN; POWDER; SLAG
AB Polymer-modified repair mortar (PMRM) has been extensively used in the construction industry for contributing to environmental sustainability, intelligent, and high-performance buildings. PMRM, due to its excellent mechanical properties and durability, has become an ideal solution for addressing the challenges of modern infrastructure repair and maintenance. The objective of this study is to explore the feasibility of incorporating fly ash (FA) and granite powder (GP) into PMRM to improve its performance and sustainability. This study also investigates the impact of three polymers-hydroxypropyl cellulose ether (CE), organic silicone powder water repellent (WR), and redispersible polymer powder (RP)-on mechanical properties and impermeability. Pore structure plays a key role in determining the performance of PMRM. Mercury Intrusion Porosimetry (MIP) was used to study the changes in the internal pore structure of the mortar, which is crucial for understanding how pore structure influences material design optimization. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was also performed on the PMRM dataset to identify the primary factors affecting its performance. In addition, a multi-input and multi-output predictive model using a 6-14-4 grey wolf optimization backpropagation neural network (GWOBPNN) architecture was designed to predict the PMRM characteristics. The dataset consisted of 744 samples, of which 60 % (446) were used for model training and 40 % (298) were used for model validation. Results indicated that the addition of 15 % FA significantly improved the gel and transition pores in the PMRM, whereas a combination of 14 % FA and 6 % GP optimized the capillary and macropores. A proportion that maximizes performance of FA and GP resulted in the most balanced pore-size distribution. Compared to other classical predictive models, the GWO-BPNN model demonstrated significant advantages in terms of fitting and predictive accuracy, and it can predict the mechanical properties and impermeability of PMRM, providing a scientific basis for the development of advanced repair materials.
