TI Prediction of Polycaprolactone Molecular Weight Synthesized via Enzymatic Polymerization Process: Comparing Training Algorithms of Artificial Neural Network Modeling
SO PROCESS INTEGRATION AND OPTIMIZATION FOR SUSTAINABILITY
LA English
DT Article
DE Artificial Neural Networks; Enzymatic Polymerization; Training Algorithms; Candida Antarctica Lipase B
ID RING-OPENING POLYMERIZATION; ANTARTICA-LIPASE B; OPTIMIZATION
AB Poly-epsilon-caprolactone (PCL), a polymer widely used in biomedical field especially as implantable biomaterial as it is easily biodegradable. It is also Food and Drug Administration approved. Currently industrial scale Poly-epsilon-caprolactone is produced using chemical methods with toxic catalysts. Moreover, due to its metallic residues, employability of PCL is restricted. More often high temperatures and harsh conditions are needed in chemical PCL synthesis. To address these issues, Poly-epsilon-caprolactone was synthesized from epsilon-caprolactone (epsilon-CL) with Candida Antarctica Lipase B (CALB) as catalyst, which are non-toxic, easy to process and has mild operating conditions. The weight average molecular weight (M-w) and number average molecular weight (M-n) are the experimental results. In this study, Feedforward Artificial Neural Networks (FANN) model is developed using reaction time, reaction temperature, mixing speed and monomer/solvent ratio as the operating conditions to predict number average molecular weight and molecular weight. Four training algorithms of FANN were used and Bayesian Regularization (BR) showed better performance over other training algorithms with mean squared error (MSE), mean absolute error (MAE) and mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) values of 0.073, 0.240 and 0.00013, respectively, for number average molecular weight and 0.052, 0.229 and 0.00008, respectively, for molecular weight. Also, root mean squared error (RMSE), coefficient of determinant (R-2) and standard error of prediction (SEP) values are 0.688, 1.00 and 0.031, respectively, for the number average molecular weight and 0.688, 1 and 0.022 for molecular weight. Overall, the developed FANN model was successful in predicting the molecular weight of the polymer.
