TI Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy and nanoscale characterization of conductive polymer composites for real-time skin Interface sensing in AI-driven affective computing
SO MICROCHEMICAL JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Conductive polymer nanocomposites; Skin impedance sensing; Micro-characterization; AI-driven emotion mapping; Stimulus-response systems; Machine learning signal analysis; Electrodermal activity; Intelligent biosensing systems
AB The rapid development of intelligent healthcare technologies has led to the increased use of functional nanomaterials, including electrochemical sensors, for real-time physiological monitoring. This paper reports the results of our research project focusing on the use of electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) to characterize and evaluate the sensing performance of conductive polymer composite materials designed for real-time monitoring of skin interfaces in artificial intelligence (AI)-based affective computing applications. The conductive polymer composite materials consist of a matrix of poly 3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene (PEDOT) and either graphene nanoplatelets or multi-walled carbon nanotubes, which contribute to increased electrical conductivity, mechanical flexibility, and stable electrode-skin interfaces during dynamic physiological changes. Nanoscale images obtained using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM) demonstrated the uniform dispersion of the carbon nano-fillers in the composite matrix and supported the creation of interconnective conductive pathways that will have a significant impact on charge transfer characteristics and impedance sensing sensitivity. An experiment to study the interaction of electrical current with chemical reaction produced an improved method for analyzing the effect of the skin's electrical resistance in real time, while also allowing the user to have access to instantaneous measurements of their emotional responses. By having improved sensitivity, lower noise levels, faster response times, and greater stability, these new materials have proven to be more reliable than traditional ones. The use of a convolutional neural network for the extraction of impedance features from the electrochemical signal improves classification accuracy of the subjects' affective states via machine learning algorithms. To improve the accuracy of the interpretation of electrical signals associated with emotional/affective responses, artificial intelligence (AI) was used as a support analytical method to assess the quality of the chemical signals created by the sensor. This combination of electrochemical sensing and nanoscale material engineering combined with AI-supported analysis establishes a strategy for developing sustainable and energy efficient intelligent biosensing systems. The proposed method will facilitate the growth of sustainable smart materials and wearable health technology that can potentially be utilized in fields such as tailor-made medicine, human-machine interaction, and adaptive neurophysiological monitoring
