TI Quantitative assessment framework of print quality in direct ink writing of magnetoactive elastomers using image processing: effects of rheology and processing parameters
SO JOURNAL OF MANUFACTURING PROCESSES
LA English
DT Article
DE Direct ink writing; Magnetoactive elastomers; Rheology; Image processing; Print quality; Shape fidelity
AB Magnetoactive elastomers (MAEs) have garnered significant interest as versatile candidates for soft robotics, sensing, actuation, energy storage, and energy harvesting due to their ability to deform in response to magnetic fields. While MAEs offer immense potential for adaptive and functional applications, fabricating complex structures requires precise control over both the rheological properties of the dispersions and the processing parameters to ensure printability and structural integrity in direct ink writing (DIW). A successful direct ink writing of MAEs remains challenging due to the interplay between material composition, extrusion behavior, and print quality. In this study, we develop a systematic framework for optimizing the DIW of MAEs by investigating the interplay between rheology and printing parameters. Using polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) as the elastomer matrix and iron oxide (Fe3O4) particles as the magnetic filler, we conducted a comprehensive analysis of the rheological behavior of PDMS dispersions loaded with surface-treated iron oxide to identify optimal ink formulations with desirable yield stress and shear-thinning behavior. Then, a grid-based parametric DIW study systematically explored the effects of composition, shear rate, nozzle speed, and z-offset on print quality and shape fidelity, demonstrated using two ink compositions. To quantitatively assess the impact of these parameters, image processing techniques were employed to evaluate print quality and shape fidelity over the crosslinking time. Then, an artificial neural network regressor was applied to the print quality datasets to determine the relative contribution of each input parameter on the print quality. By correlating rheological parameters with print quality metrics, this study establishes a robust methodology for fine-tuning DIW parameters, enabling precise and reproducible fabrication of MAEs. Furthermore, the framework developed in this research for characterizing print quality is agnostic of material composition and DIW technique, making it broadly applicable to a wide range of 3D printing processes for assessing print layers. This approach enhances the efficiency of additive manufacturing by minimizing trial-and-error iterations and material waste, ultimately advancing the field of functional elastomeric composites for applications in soft robotics, biomedical devices, and adaptive structures.
