TI A unified deep learning framework for phase demodulation in off-axis digital holographic interferometry: applications in polymer fiber characterization and 3D imaging
SO JOURNAL OF OPTICS
LA English
DT Article
DE digital holographic interferometry; phase demodulation; convolutional neural networks; polymeric fiber characterization; opto-mechanical characterization; holographic tomography
AB Off-axis digital holographic interferometry is a powerful non-destructive technique for quantitative phase imaging, yet conventional reconstruction methods face substantial computational challenges when processing complex experimental scenarios. This study presents a developed convolutional neural network framework for direct phase demodulation from off-axis holographic interference patterns, eliminating the need for traditional multi-step reconstruction procedures. This suggested method advances real-time opto-mechanical experiments without requiring explicit knowledge of propagation distances between focus and defocus conditions. The network architecture incorporates multi-scale encoder pathways, focus-adaptive processing, and distance-agnostic decoding to handle four primary holographic scenarios: ordinary off-axis holograms, axially tilted samples with mixed focus conditions, angularly misaligned specimens, and combined multi-distance and angular variations. The framework was trained and validated using experimental 1000 high-resolution holographic patterns from diverse polymeric fiber specimens. Experimental validation demonstrated exceptional performance with validation PSNR of 25.66 dB and SSIM of 0.903, while reducing processing time from 9-16 min to 30-48 s per image. The network eliminates the need for explicit knowledge of propagation distances, focus conditions, or object orientations. Practical applications include dynamic opto-mechanical characterization of isotactic polypropylene fibers under mechanical drawing and three-dimensional morphological analysis of iPP/TiO2 nanocomposite fibers through holographic tomography. This work advances holographic metrology by providing a unified solution for quantitative phase imaging under the four experimental configurations.
