TI AI-driven chemometric deep learning for monitoring oxidative degradation in polymer sports protective materials
SO MICROCHEMICAL JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Chemometric deep learning; Artificial intelligence in materials; Machine learning-assisted spectroscopy; Data-driven materials intelligence; Smart analytical modeling
ID SENSORS
AB Sports safety gear made from polymer materials continuously faces mechanical washers, ultraviolet (UV) and oxygen exposure, and this will result in damage that causes them to degrade mechanically and negatively impact their safety, quality and life-span. Most of the traditional methods for assessing the degradation of polymer safety equipment are slow, and only give a descriptive overview of how much the material has degraded and are poor at predicting when polymer safety equipment is going to break down in the early stages. This project presents a new way to assess the formation of oxidation functional groups in polymer sports protection materials using an AI-based chemometric deep learning approach. Spectral datasets produced from fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy of polymers that have been subjected to controlled-aged conditions have been processed using advanced multivariate pre-processing techniques baseline correction, normalisation, and derivative and dimensionality-reduced by Principal Component Analysis (PCA). A 1D Convolutional Neural Network (1D-CNN) architecture has been built to extract hierarchical features from the spectral data corresponding to each oxidation functional group formed on the polymers (Carbonyl CO, Hydroxyl OH, and Peroxide OOH) during oxidative aged conditions. The developed model showed an accuracy of 96.4%, R-2 of 0.94, and RMSE of 0.012 when compared with traditional machine learning methods such as support vector-machine (SVM), random-forest (RF) or Partial Least Squares (PLS) regression. Analysis of cross-validation showed a strong ability for generalization and minimal overfitting, while the interpretability analysis showed that the model's attention weights corresponded to important spectral bands associated with oxidation (similar to 1710 cm(-1) and similar to 3400 cm(-1)) helping advance the mechanistic understanding of polymer degradation. The proposed framework goes beyond predictive ability to enable non-destructive and real-time assessment of degradation, aiding the manufacture of intelligence-based rules for determining the health of a materials' lifespan in the manufacture of sports protection equipment. This ability helps identify oxidative damage early, extending the life of materials and decreasing the volume of materials disposed of prematurely while providing a route to create more sustainable designs for polymers and improve quality control processes. This work provides a robust fusion of oxidative polymer chemistry with AI-based smart intelligence computing for monitoring sustainable materials.
