TI A review on metal-organic framework-based polymer solid-state electrolytes for energy storage
SO JOURNAL OF ENERGY STORAGE
LA English
DT Review
DE Metal-organic frameworks; Polymer solid-state electrolytes; Lithium metal batteries; Artificial intelligence; Energy storage
ID SIMS ANALYSIS; PERFORMANCE; MEMBRANE; BATTERIES; FILLER; AL
AB Solid-state electrolytes (SSEs) represent a critical advancement toward safer and higher-energy-density batteries beyond liquid systems. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-based solid polymer electrolytes (MSPEs) leverage tunable architectures to address the inherent limitations of polymer electrolytes, such as low ionic conductivity and interfacial instability. Recent progress in MSPEs design within a novel integrated artificial intelligence (AI)- simulation-experiment closed-loop framework is systematically reviewed. Advanced characterization methods used to elucidate ion transport and interfacial dynamics are summarized. Scalable manufacturing methods are evaluated, and cost analyses are provided to highlight industrial feasibility, with a focus on the cost advantages offered by deep learning-specifically reinforcement learning (RL). The significance of multiscale simulation methods, represented by DFT and MD, in unveiling electrolyte structure-property relationships is emphasized. Crucially, building on the traditional binary research paradigm of experiment-simulation, the transformative role of AI in performance prediction, high-throughput screening, and inverse material design tailored for grid-scale and electric vehicle energy storage is highlighted. This multidisciplinary approach provides practical insights and robust design paradigms for next-generation solid-state battery applications.
