TI Data-driven models toward polymer glass transition temperature embedded in structures
SO POLYMER
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymers; Monte Carlo simulation; Glass transition temperature; Group descriptor
ID NEURAL-NETWORK PREDICTION; DESCRIPTORS; QSPR
AB The glass transition temperature (Tg) is a critical property of polymers. There are small experimental data about Tg. It is necessary to establish a data-driven method for the prediction of Tg. A machine learning framework is proposed by integrating a large-scale dataset containing 1016 data points, as well as 6 machine learning models, 123 group descriptors, and 1000 Monte Carlo simulations. The R2 of Gaussian process regression in the test set is 0.9683, which is more accurate than the other five machine learning models. In addition, linear optimization was adopted to reconstruct group parameters and improve the group contribution additivity method (GAP). An external independent test set was employed to verify the universality and accuracy of machine learning and GAP methods. The proposed methods for the prediction of Tg with group structures provide good guidance for polymer molecule design and application.
