PT J
AU Xu, H
   Zou, XX
   Sneed, LH
AF Xu, Hao
   Zou, Xingxing
   Sneed, Lesley H.
TI A two-stage classification-regression method for prediction of flexural strength of fiber reinforced polymer strengthened reinforced concrete beams
SO ENGINEERING APPLICATIONS OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE Classification-regression method; Fiber reinforced polymer; Flexural strength; Machine learning; Reinforced concrete
ID SHEAR-STRESS CONCENTRATION; RC BEAMS; DEBONDING FAILURE; CFRP SHEETS; BEHAVIOR; CARBON; PLATES; CONFIGURATION; PERFORMANCE; MODELS
AB Accurate prediction of the flexural strength of externally bonding fiber reinforced polymer strengthened reinforced concrete beams (FSRCBs) is challenging for both traditional semi-empirical equations and machine learning models because it involves classification of failure modes in advance. Machine learning models that can predict the flexural strength without knowing the failure modes are scare to date. This study proposed a novel two-stage machine learning, classification-regression method, to first predict the failure mode of FSRCBs and then use the predicted failure mode as an additional input to predict the flexural strength. Using a database of 702 FSRCBs collected from the literature, the accuracy of the failure mode predicted by the proposed model reached 96.5%. With the predicted failure mode, the XGBoost (extreme gradient boosting) and AdaBoost (adaptive boosting) regression model were used to predict the flexural strength with an R2 of 0.966 and 0.973, respectively. Shapley additive explanation (SHAP) analysis was employed to ensure interpretability of the predictions generated by the proposed method. An explicit and practical equation was derived to predict the flexural strength based on results of machine learning, which showed higher prediction accuracy compared with traditional equations. A graphic users' interface with the well-trained model is also provided.
