TI Machine learning-base analysis and prediction of non-isothermal viscoelastic polymer film casting process
SO PHYSICS OF FLUIDS
LA English
DT Article
ID NECK-IN PHENOMENON; 3-DIMENSIONAL SIMULATION; EXTENSIONAL RHEOLOGY; DEBORAH NUMBER; ALGORITHMS; FLUIDS; MODEL; FLOW
AB Predicting film thickness and width is of critical importance in polymer film casting process as they are key indicators reflecting rheological defects such as neck-in phenomenon and edge bead effect. Recently, our direct numerical simulations [Gong et al., Phys. Fluids 37, 093104 (2025)] have revealed that heat transfer coefficient, viscosity, and relaxation time are key factors affecting film dimensions in film casting, consistent with experimental observations. Building on this foundation, this work adopts a data-driven approach to develop predictive models. Machine learning algorithms can make predictions by self-learning from pre-existing experimental or computational data, and progressively improve prediction accuracy with minimal, if any, human intervention. In this paper, a dataset of about 200 polymer film casting cases is generated by means of numerical simulation, and the performances of six common machine learning models are systematically evaluated and compared in the task of film thickness and width prediction. The results show that the gradient boosting decision tree model exhibits optimal prediction ability under complex conditions. This model is able to predict final film thickness and width with high accuracy (correlation coefficient R-2 > 0.99). Additionally, relaxation time is found to have the greatest effect on the prediction results through significance analysis. On this basis, a new machine learning model is developed through hyperparameter tuning, which further improves the prediction accuracy. This study extends the application of machine learning methods in the field of film casting, which is conducive to optimizing the processing procedure and reducing material waste.
