TI Predicting possible polymer sequences based on given adhesive free energies of polymer-surface interactions using deep learning
SO CHEMICAL PHYSICS LETTERS
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymer sequences; Prediction; Deep learning
ID COMPUTATIONAL NEURAL-NETWORKS; CHALLENGES; SIMULATION; DYNAMICS
AB Polymer structure design plays a pivotal role in a wide range of biological and industrial applications, particularly where precise control over surface adhesion is critical. In this study, we present a data-driven deep learning framework for the inverse design of polymer sequences with given target adhesive free energy (Delta T) on functionalized surfaces, using a conditional generative adversarial networks. Empirical evaluations demonstrate the predictive accuracy, robustness, and generalization capabilities of the framework. This work marks a novel solution in the application of deep learning for functional polymer design. It transitions from conventional forward modeling to scalable inverse sequence prediction, thereby enabling molecular-level control in bottom-up manufacturing and offering a generalizable pathway for the tailored property design of polymeric materials.
