TI An efficient framework to design near-IR monomers for polymer solar cells with the help of machine learning, virtual screening and chemical space visualization
SO JOURNAL OF PHOTOCHEMISTRY AND PHOTOBIOLOGY A-CHEMISTRY
LA English
DT Article
DE Organic Solar cells; Machine learning; Data analysis; Monomers; Cluster analysis
ID NONLINEAR-OPTICAL PROPERTIES; ABSORPTION-SPECTRA; COMPLEXES; DYES
AB Organic solar cells (OSCs) have emerged as attractive alternatives for capturing solar energy. In the research work, the information from literature was collected to create a database of 700 organic semiconductors and machine learning (ML) was performed. Numerous organic molecules were designed. The chosen monomers were then subjected to cluster analysis. The silhoutte data including t-SNE data plot predict similar and dissimilar data points. In order to determine the structural diversity and similarity of the chosen monomers, chemical similarity analysis using RDkit is employed to visualize the chemical space. The hierarchical relationship and graphical representation between several groups of data was depicted using cluster graph and heat map. Finally, the synthesis accessibility of designed monomers was analyzed using Synthetic Bayesian classifier (SYBA). Through the integration of machine learning, cluster analysis, and synthetic accessibility, the methodology offers a productive and organized framework for designing polymers for OSCs.
