TI Enhanced understanding of bond strength of fiber-reinforcement-polymer bars reinforced concrete subjected to elevated temperature for interfacial failure prevention: Explainable artificial intelligence method
SO ENGINEERING APPLICATIONS OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE Failure analysis; Bond strength; Fiber-reinforcement-polymer bars; Concrete structures; Machine learning; Elevated temperature
ID MECHANICAL-PROPERTIES; TENSILE PERFORMANCE; SHEAR-STRENGTH; FRP COMPOSITES; REBARS
AB Fiber-reinforcement-polymer (FRP) bars, as alternatives to traditional steel bars, effectively enhance the durability of concrete structures in extreme environments. However, the interfacial mechanical performance of FRP bars reinforced concrete degrades under elevated temperatures or fire, leading to bonded interface failure. The bond strength between FRP bars and concrete is critical to structural performance, but existing studies lack accurate prediction of its evolution under multi-factor coupling and clear mechanistic explanations. In order to accurately characterize the interfacial bond performance of FRP-reinforced concrete under elevated temperatures, three machine learning models, namely Support Vector Machine (SVM), Random Forest (RF) and eXterme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), are established to predict bond strength using a database of 289 experimental results. These models are selected for their fitting capabilities matching the data's complexity (nonlinear coupling of multiple features): SVM handles moderate nonlinearity, RF adapts to mid-to-high complexity with anti-overfitting, and XGBoost captures intricate interactions for high complexity. Comparing them identifies the best-fitted model for bond strength prediction. In the established models based on the machine learning algorithms, the significant features are selected such as experimental temperature (T), diameter of FRP bar (d), anchorage length (la), concrete compressive strength (fc), and concrete cover thickness to diameter of FRP bar ratio (c/d) considering the credibility of black-box models and the rationality of input features. In addition, the SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) method is employed to enhance interpretability and overcome the "black box" problem of the machine learning (ML) based prediction models. Beyond explaining model behavior, its application here provides actionable insights for fire-resistant design: it quantifies feature contributions and interactive effects, addressing the lack of mechanistic guidance in previous artificial intelligence (AI) based studies. This paper provides meaningful guidelines for the interfacial failure prevention and safe service of FRP bars reinforced concrete structures in the elevated temperature environment.
