TI Natural macromolecular Core-Shell matrix modification and machine learning prediction boosts biocompatibility of polyethersulfone membrane
SO JOURNAL OF MEMBRANE SCIENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE Polyethersulfone; Biocompatibility; Biomolecules; Core-shell nanoparticles; Machine learning
ID NANOPARTICLES; PVP
AB The biocompatibility of polymer membranes is still persisting issue, especially for polyethersulfone-based membranes. In this regard, new material design based on biomolecules combined with inorganic nanoparticles sparks high potential in enhancing polyethersulfone membrane's biocompatibility. This research introduces a distinctive strategy that combines the integration of core-shell hydrophilic additives, including silicon oxide nanoparticles and ferro ferric oxide nanoparticles sandwiched by polyvinylpyrrolidone polymer and modified with naturally sourced macromolecules. This strategy allows the achievement of complex system that guarantees guided functionality, abundancy, and an environmentally friendly approach for polyethersulfone membrane modification. The preparation of the membrane includes using various material compositions to examine the impact of additives on the performance of the membrane. Accordingly, the modified membranes show significant improvement in water permeability up to 600 and 810 L m(-2) L-1. Moreover, all modified membranes demonstrate high biocompatibility in terms of hemolysis rate (<0.5 %), less platelet adhesion, and prolonged blood clotting time. This research also conducted in light of building a machine learning model to predict novel membrane performance features, and prove that pairing creative material design with machine learning tools can be highly effective in improving polyethersulfone membrane biocompatibility for bio-based applications.
