TI Machine learning-aided high-temperature polymer composites for capacitive energy storage
SO NANO ENERGY
LA English
DT Article
DE Machine learning; Polymer composites; High-temperature energy storage; Dielectric capacitors
ID IRREVERSIBLE-PROCESSES
AB Polymer film capacitors are widely employed in advanced electric and electrical systems due to their high-power density and high operating voltage. However, the energy storage performance of polymer dielectrics deteriorates drastically at elevated temperatures, which severely restricts their large-scale application in harsh environments. To address this challenge, we employ machine learning (ML) to screen desired functional small-molecule fillers to design high-temperature-resistant polymer composites. A light gradient boosting machine (LightGBM) model is constructed to predict the key properties of small molecules and polyetherimide (PEI)-based composites, which are further evaluated using five criteria of high dielectric constant (High-kappa), low free volume fraction (Low-FFV), high thermal conductivity (High-lambda), low lowest unoccupied molecular orbital energy level (Low-ELUMO) and High-ELUMO. Subsequently, 15 representative filtered small molecules are selected for the experimental preparation of mono-component composites, among which PEI-9,9 '-bifluorenyl (BF) achieves a 178% improvement of discharged energy density Ud over pure PEI. Furthermore, a multi-component synergistic strategy is proposed and experimentally validated, where PEI-anthracene (AE)/triphenylene (TPHEN)/BF composite achieves a Ud of 7.0 J cm- 3 at 150 degrees C with an efficiency eta of 90%. This work realizes machine learning-assisted screening and rational design of high-temperature polymer composite dielectrics, and puts forward an effective concept for the synergistic regulation effect of multi-component small molecules in polymer composites.
