DT Review; Early Access
ID PREDICTION; OPTIMIZATION; GENOME
AB Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping performance-driven polymer design by connecting molecular structures, physical mechanisms, processing conditions, and application-level objectives within an integrated design framework. Unlike small molecules, polymer properties are governed not only by repeat-unit chemistry but also by molecular weight and dispersity, chain architecture, morphology, processing history, and operating environment. These challenges are particularly pronounced in polar and charged polymers, where electrostatics, ion association, hydration, hydrogen bonding, and interfacial phenomena collectively determine functionality. This review presents an AI ecosystem for performance-driven polymer design, covering data infrastructures, molecular representations, descriptor hierarchies, property-learning models, physics-informed and multiscale learning, inverse-design strategies, and emerging AI-agent workflows. Representative applications are discussed across high-performance polymers, polymer electrolytes, polymer brushes and interfacial layers, and polyelectrolytes for solar interfacial evaporation, highlighting different levels of AI maturity and application readiness. Across these systems, AI provides complementary capabilities for property prediction, mechanism-aware learning, multi-objective optimization, and closed-loop materials design. Future progress will depend on standardized datasets, transferable descriptors, uncertainty-aware optimization, and tighter integration of simulations, experiments, and autonomous workflows. By combining data, physical knowledge, and iterative validation, AI can provide a practical design engine for next-generation functional polymers.
